+##### onRetry(context)
+
+Type: `Function`
+
+Callback invoked only when another attempt is going to happen, right before the wait. It receives the same context object as `onFailedAttempt`. Unlike `onFailedAttempt`, it is not called for the final failure, so it suits retry metrics.
+
+The function is called _after_ `onFailedAttempt`, `shouldConsumeRetry`, and `shouldRetry`, and is _not_ called when any of them ends the retries. It is also not called on `AbortError` or on an abort through `signal`.
+
+The function can return a promise, which is awaited before the wait starts. If it throws, all retries will be aborted and the original promise will reject with the thrown error.
+
+```js
+import pRetry from 'p-retry';
+
+const run = async () => { … };
+
+const result = await pRetry(run, {
+	onRetry: ({attemptNumber, retryDelay}) => {
+		metrics.increment('retries');
+	}
+});
+```
+
 ##### shouldRetry(context)
 
 Type: `Function`
@@ -195,6 +217,15 @@ A delay is a whole number of milliseconds, so a `minTimeout` below `0.5` makes t
 > [!WARNING]
 > A delay of `0` schedules no timer at all. If the operation also fails without waiting on a timer or I/O, attempts run back to back without ever yielding to the event loop. Combined with `retries: Infinity`, that is a busy loop that keeps the CPU pinned and stops timers, I/O, and `signal` from ever running. Use a small non-zero value if the operation can fail indefinitely.
 
+##### initialDelay
+
+Type: `number`\
+Default: `0`
+
+The number of milliseconds to wait before the first attempt.
+
+The wait is not part of `maxRetryTime`, which starts counting when the first attempt does. If `signal` aborts during the wait, the promise rejects with the abort reason and the operation is never called. A fractional value rounds to a whole millisecond, and a value of `0` schedules no timer.
+
 ##### maxTimeout
 
 Type: `number`\
